  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 330 PM EDT TUE AUG 18 2026\n\nAt the onset of the extended forecast period, large-scale 500 mb \ntroughing remains anchored across much of the eastern CONUS. In the \npre-dawn hours Thursday, a nocturnal 40-50 kt low-level jet (LLJ) \noriented across western and central Kentucky will coincide with a \nshortwave passing through the Ohio Valley. While the LLJ weakens to \n30-40 kts as it shifts into eastern Kentucky, rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms are expected through Thursday. The first round will \nmove through during the morning hours, favoring areas across the \nCumberland, Southern Licking, and Big Sandy River basins near the \nTN, VA, and WV borders. A brief lull in activity is expected before \nredevelopment occurs early Thursday afternoon along the I-64 \ncorridor, propagating south through the remainder of the day. Models \nindicate drier air working in aloft by late afternoon, which could \nsuppress rain chances. However, the environment will support PWAT \nvalues near 1.75 inches (80th to 85th percentile of climatology). \nGiven saturated soils from recent rainfall, isolated instances of \nminor flooding remain possible Thursday through Thursday night, \nsupporting WPCs Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall. \nAdequate storm motion should keep activity progressive, limiting \nwidespread flash flooding potential.\n\nAs the frontal boundary associated with Thursdays convection lingers \nnear the area on Friday, shower and thunderstorm chances will \npersist, with the highest probabilities during the afternoon along \nand south of the Hal Rogers Parkway / KY Highway 80 corridor. Beyond \nFriday, models and ensemble guidance remain in broad agreement on a \ncontinued upper-level pattern featuring a ridge over the western \nCONUS and troughing across the east, though amplitude differences \nremain. Another shortwave embedded within the longwave trough is \nprojected to dig out of the Upper Midwest into the Great Lakes and \nOhio Valley Saturday into Sunday. Multiple 00Z AI-based guidance \nsuites from August 18th show conditional severe potential on \nSaturday, primarily centered over SW Pennsylvania, with lower \nprobabilities (5-15 percent) extending southwest into eastern \nKentucky. At present, PoPs support isolated to scattered coverage \nacross the Licking and Big Sandy basins on Saturday. Isolated shower \nor storm activity may linger across the Big Sandy early Sunday \nmorning; otherwise, dry weather is expected to return through Monday \nmorning. By Monday afternoon, guidance depicts another upper level \nlow which a few models attempt to close off digging southeast from \nthe Upper Midwest into the Northern Great Lakes. This feature could \nbring another round of shower and thunderstorm chances to the area \non Tuesday.",
